<p dir=3D"auto">The primary difference is that <code>var x =3D 1</code> exp=
licitly declares a function-scoped or globally-scoped variable, whereas <co=
de>x =3D 1</code> assigns a value to an identifier without declaring it, wh=
ich can lead to global scope pollution or runtime errors depending on your =
code's strictness mode</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><code>var x =3D 1</code>:</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>Allowed in 'strict mode'.</li>
<li>The var statement declares a function-scoped or globally-scoped variabl=
e, optionally initializing it to a value.</li>
<li>Variables declared using <code>var</code> inside a <code>{ }</code> blo=
ck can be accessed from outside the block.</li>
<li>Variables defined using <code>var</code> inside a function are not acce=
ssible (visible) from outside the function.</li>
<li>Duplicate variable declarations using <code>var</code> will not trigger=
 an error, even in strict mode, and the variable will not lose its value un=
less another assignment is performed.</li>
</ul>
