<p dir=3D"auto">There are eight basic data types in JavaScript.</p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table data-catalyst=3D""><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Data Types</th>
<th>Description</th>
<th>Example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>String</td>
<td>Represents textual data</td>
<td>let str =3D 'Hi', let str2 =3D "Hello", let str3 =3D `Hello World`</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Number</td>
<td>An integer or a floating-point number</td>
<td>let num =3D 3, let num2 =3D 3.234, let num3 =3D 3e-2</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>BigInt</td>
<td>An integer with arbitrary precision</td>
<td>let num =3D 900719925124740999n, let num =3D 1n</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Boolean</td>
<td>Any of two values: true or false</td>
<td>let flag =3D true</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>undefined</td>
<td>A data type whose variable is not initialized</td>
<td>let a;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>null</td>
<td>Denotes a null value</td>
<td>let a =3D null;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Symbol</td>
<td>Data type whose instances are unique and immutable</td>
<td>let value =3D Symbol('hello');</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Object</td>
<td>key-value pairs of collection of data</td>
<td>let student =3D { };</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>String:</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><code>String</code> is used to store text. In JavaScript, s=
trings are surrounded by quotes:</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>Single quotes: 'Hello'</li>
<li>Double quotes: "Hello"</li>
<li>Backticks: `Hello`</li>
</ul>
