<p dir=3D"auto">In javaScript, functions can be stored as a variable inside=
 an object or an array as well as it can be passed as an argument or be ret=
urned by another function. That makes function <strong>first-class function=
</strong> in JavaScript.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">A <strong>first-class function</strong> means functions are=
 treated as values =E2=80=94 they can be:</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li><strong>Assigned to variables</strong>: <code>const greet =3D function(=
) { return "Hello"; }</code></li>
<li><strong>Passed as arguments</strong>: <code>arr.map(fn)</code></li>
<li><strong>Returned from other functions</strong>: <code>function outer() =
{ return function inner() {} }</code></li>
<li><strong>Stored in data structures</strong>: <code>const obj =3D { fn: (=
) =3D&gt; {} }</code></li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"auto">JavaScript fully supports first-class functions, which enab=
les patterns like callbacks, higher-order functions, and closures.</p>
