<p dir=3D"auto">The <code>this</code> keyword refers to an <code>object</co=
de>. Which object depends on how this is being invoked (used or called). Th=
e <code>this</code> keyword refers to different objects depending on how it=
 is used.</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>In an object method, <code>this</code> refers to the object.</li>
<li>Alone, <code>this</code> refers to the global object.</li>
<li>In a function, <code>this</code> refers to the global object.</li>
<li>In a function, in strict mode, <code>this</code> is <code>undefined</co=
de>.</li>
<li>In an event, <code>this</code> refers to the element that received the =
event.</li>
<li>Methods like <code>call()</code>, <code>apply()</code>, and <code>bind(=
)</code> can refer <code>this</code> to any object.</li>
</ul>
