the relational operators <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=3D</code>, <code>&gt=
;</code>, <code>&gt;=3D</code> can be used to compare JavaScript dates.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">However, the equality operators <code>=3D=3D</code>, <code>=
!=3D</code>, <code>=3D=3D=3D</code>, <code>!=3D=3D</code> cannot be used to=
 compare (the value of) dates because:</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>Two distinct objects are never equal for either strict or abstract comp=
arisons.</li>
<li>An expression comparing Objects is only true if the operands reference =
the same Object.</li>
</ul>
