<li><strong>Function declaration</strong> (<code>function foo() {}</code>):=
 The entire function =E2=80=94 name <strong>and</strong> body =E2=80=94 is =
hoisted. The function is callable anywhere in its scope, even before the li=
ne it appears on.</li>
<li><strong>Function expression</strong> (<code>const foo =3D function() {}=
</code>): Only the variable declaration is hoisted (if <code>var</code>); t=
he assignment =E2=80=94 the function value itself =E2=80=94 is not. Calling=
 it before the line throws <code>TypeError</code> (not <code>ReferenceError=
</code>) because the variable exists but holds <code>undefined</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Arrow function</strong> (<code>const foo =3D () =3D&gt; {}</cod=
e>): Same as function expression =E2=80=94 it is always assigned to a varia=
ble, so the same rules apply.</li>
</ul>
