<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Real-World Use Case:</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto">React's functional components and hooks use arrow functions=
 almost exclusively for event handlers passed as JSX props (<code>onClick=
=3D{() =3D&gt; dispatch(action)}</code>). Because these arrow functions inh=
erit <code>this</code> from the component scope (which in a functional comp=
onent is irrelevant =E2=80=94 there is no <code>this</code>), the lexical b=
inding is harmless and the concise syntax reduces boilerplate. Conversely, =
in Express.js route handlers or Mongoose middleware that rely on <code>this=
</code> referring to the document/request, using arrow functions is a commo=
n production bug =E2=80=94 the official Mongoose docs explicitly warn again=
st it.</p>
