<p dir=3D"auto">Errors in <code>async</code> functions are handled with sta=
ndard <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>/<code>finally</code> blocks. An a=
waited Promise that rejects throws at the <code>await</code> expression, wh=
ich <code>catch</code> intercepts exactly like a synchronous throw.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Key pitfalls:</strong></p>
<ol dir=3D"auto">
<li><strong>Forgetting <code>await</code></strong> =E2=80=94 the Promise is=
 created but not awaited; errors are silently swallowed.</li>
<li><strong>Missing <code>catch</code></strong> =E2=80=94 an unhandled reje=
ction in Node.js (since v15) causes the process to exit with a non-zero cod=
e; in browsers it fires <code>window.unhandledrejection</code>.</li>
<li><strong><code>try/catch</code> around non-awaited Promises</strong> =E2=
=80=94 <code>try</code> only catches synchronous throws and awaited rejecti=
ons, not fire-and-forget Promises inside the block.</li>
<li><strong>Partial failure in loops</strong> =E2=80=94 <code>await</code> =
inside a <code>for</code> loop is sequential; one failure aborts all subseq=
uent iterations unless each is individually caught.</li>
</ol>
