<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Difference:</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto">The primary difference between using <code>{}</code> and <c=
ode>new Object()</code> to create an object is that the former uses object =
literal syntax while the latter uses object constructor syntax.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">Object literals are more concise and easier to read and wri=
te, especially when creating objects with a small number of properties. On =
the other hand, object constructors are more flexible and can be used to cr=
eate objects with properties that are not known at the time of object creat=
ion.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">In general, it is recommended to use object literal syntax =
(<code>{}</code>) for creating objects unless there is a specific reason to=
 use the object constructor syntax (<code>new Object()</code>).</p>
