<p dir=3D"auto">A <strong>higher-order function (HOF)</strong> is a functio=
n that either:</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li><strong>Takes one or more functions as arguments</strong>, or</li>
<li><strong>Returns a function</strong> as its result.</li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"auto">This is possible in JavaScript because functions are <stron=
g>first-class citizens</strong> =E2=80=94 they can be stored in variables, =
passed as arguments, and returned from other functions.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><code>Array.prototype.map</code>, <code>filter</code>, and =
<code>reduce</code> are the three canonical HOFs in JavaScript. They are <s=
trong>pure transformations</strong> =E2=80=94 they do not mutate the origin=
al array; they return new arrays or values.</p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table data-catalyst=3D""><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Method</th>
<th>Input</th>
<th>Output</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>map(fn)</code></td>
<td>Array of A</td>
<td>Array of B (same length)</td>
<td>Transform each element</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>filter(fn)</code></td>
<td>Array of A</td>
<td>Array of A (=E2=89=A4 original length)</td>
<td>Select elements matching a predicate</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>reduce(fn, init)</code></td>
<td>Array of A</td>
<td>Single value of any type</td>
<td>Accumulate/fold elements into one result</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
