<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Function composition</strong> is the mathematical c=
oncept of chaining functions such that the output of one becomes the input =
of the next: <code>(f =E2=88=98 g)(x) =3D f(g(x))</code>. In JavaScript thi=
s is implemented with two conventions:</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li><strong><code>compose(f, g, h)</code></strong> =E2=80=94 applies right-=
to-left: <code>f(g(h(x)))</code> (mathematical convention)</li>
<li><strong><code>pipe(f, g, h)</code></strong> =E2=80=94 applies left-to-r=
ight: <code>h(g(f(x)))</code> (more readable for imperative programmers)</l=
i>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"auto">Both require <strong>unary functions</strong> (single argum=
ent) for clean composition. Multi-argument functions must be curried first.=
</p>
