<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Currying</strong> transforms a function of <code>n<=
/code> arguments into a chain of <code>n</code> unary (single-argument) fun=
ctions: <code>f(a, b, c)</code> becomes <code>f(a)(b)(c)</code>. Each call =
returns a new function waiting for the next argument until all arguments ar=
e supplied.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Partial application</strong> fixes some arguments o=
f a function, returning a new function that expects the remaining arguments=
 =E2=80=94 the result does not have to be unary.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">The distinction:</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>Currying always produces unary functions step by step.</li>
<li>Partial application fixes <code>k</code> of <code>n</code> args at once=
, producing a function of <code>n-k</code> args (which can be &gt; 1).</li>
<li><code>.bind(null, arg1)</code> is partial application, not currying.</l=
i>
</ul>
