<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>2. Native Private Fields (The Modern Way)</strong><=
/p>
<p dir=3D"auto">ES2022 introduced <strong>true private class fields</strong=
> using the <code>#</code> prefix. These are enforced by the JavaScript eng=
ine and methods remain efficiently shared on the prototype. Accessing <code=
>instance.#field</code> from outside the class is a <code>SyntaxError</code=
>, not just <code>undefined</code>. This is fundamentally different from:</=
p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li><strong><code>_</code> naming convention</strong> (<code>this._value</c=
ode>) =E2=80=94 purely cosmetic, still fully public.</li>
<li><strong>Closure-based privacy</strong> (Module Pattern / WeakMap) =E2=
=80=94 private by scope, but awkward with classes and inheritance.</li>
<li><strong>TypeScript <code>private</code></strong> =E2=80=94 compile-time=
 only; the compiled JavaScript has no runtime enforcement.</li>
</ul>
<markdown-accessiblity-table data-catalyst=3D""><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Mechanism</th>
<th>Runtime enforced</th>
<th>Works with <code>in</code> operator</th>
<th>Inheritable</th>
<th>Performant</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>#</code> private field</td>
<td>Yes</td>
<td>Yes (<code>#field in obj</code>)</td>
<td>No (each class owns its own)</td>
<td>Yes (V8 optimises as struct fields)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>_</code> convention</td>
<td>No</td>
<td>N/A</td>
<td>Yes</td>
<td>Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Closure / WeakMap</td>
<td>Yes</td>
<td>No</td>
<td>No</td>
<td>Moderate (WeakMap lookup)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>TypeScript <code>private</code></td>
<td>No (compile-time only)</td>
<td>N/A</td>
<td>Yes</td>
<td>Yes</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
