<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Memoization</strong> is a caching technique that st=
ores the return value of a pure function keyed by its arguments. On subsequ=
ent calls with the same arguments, the cached result is returned without re=
-executing the function. It is the programmatic application of the mathemat=
ical concept that pure functions are referentially transparent (same input =
=E2=86=92 same output).</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Production concerns beyond the na=C3=AFve implement=
ation:</strong></p>
<ol dir=3D"auto">
<li><strong>Cache key strategy</strong> =E2=80=94 <code>JSON.stringify</cod=
e> fails for non-serialisable args (functions, circular refs, Symbols, <cod=
e>undefined</code>). A WeakMap keyed on object identity avoids this.</li>
<li><strong>Cache eviction</strong> =E2=80=94 unbounded caches grow without=
 limit. LRU (Least Recently Used) eviction is standard.</li>
<li><strong>Async memoization</strong> =E2=80=94 memoize the Promise itself=
, not the resolved value, to prevent duplicate in-flight requests.</li>
<li><strong>TTL (Time To Live)</strong> =E2=80=94 stale cached values must =
expire for data that changes over time.</li>
</ol>
