<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Comparison: Module Pattern vs ES6 Modules</strong><=
/p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table data-catalyst=3D""><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>IIFE Module Pattern</th>
<th>ES6 Modules (<code>import</code>/<code>export</code>)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Scope isolation</td>
<td>Function scope (closure)</td>
<td>File scope (native)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Dependency management</td>
<td>Manual (script order)</td>
<td>Declarative (<code>import</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Tree-shaking</td>
<td>Not supported</td>
<td>Supported by bundlers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Singleton</td>
<td>Yes (single IIFE execution)</td>
<td>Yes (module cache)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Static analysis</td>
<td>No</td>
<td>Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Server-side (Node.js)</td>
<td>Yes</td>
<td>Yes (<code>.mjs</code> or <code>"type":"module"</code>)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Use case:</strong> The IIFE pattern is still useful=
 in environments without a bundler (CDN scripts, browser extensions, legacy=
 codebases). For all modern projects, prefer ES6 modules.</p>
